     "text": "Printed by: ROI_34 11/09/2026\nCONFIDENTIAL - Contains PHI\nHill Jones and Johnston Medical Group | Dr. Pamela Howard, gastroenterology\nPatient: Jaime King DOB: 02/01/1966 Sex: F MRN: 556863\n\nPROBLEM LIST\n - Hepatitis C (SVR achieved; last tx 0.8 yrs ago) [resolved] onset 10/2017\n\nMEDICATIONS\n glecaprevir-pibrentasvir 100/40 mg\n\nENCOUNTERS\n--- 06/24/2025 Office Visit ---\nS: Patient Jaime King, a 59-year-old female, for follow-up of her Hepatitis C, which was previously diagnosed in October 2017. The patient reports no new symptoms and no complaints of fatigue, jaundice, or abdominal pain. The patient has achieved sustained virologic response (SVR) with her current treatment regimen, and she has been stable for the past 8 months.\n\nO: On physical examination, the patient appears well and in no acute distress. Vital signs are as follows: Blood pressure is 141/88 mmHg, and weight is 142 pounds. The cardiovascular exam reveals a regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. The abdominal exam is benign with no hepatosplenomegaly or tenderness. The rest of the physical exam is unremarkable.\n\nA: The patient's Hepatitis C is stable and under control with no active viral replication detected in the most recent lab tests. The patient has achieved sustained virologic response (SVR) with the current treatment regimen and has been in remission for approximately 8 months. \n\nP: The patient will continue her current treatment with glecaprevir-pibrentasvir 100/40 mg. I recommend that the patient continue her routine follow-up every 3 to 6 months to monitor for any potential relapse or complications. The patient should also maintain a healthy lifestyle, avoid alcohol, and follow up with her primary care provider for routine screenings and preventive care.\n\n--- 08/25/2024 Office Visit ---\nS: Patient Jaime King, a 58-year-old female, presents for follow-up of her Hepatitis C, which was previously diagnosed in October 2017. The patient repo