     "page": 1,
     "text": "**CLINICAL SUMMARY - Murillo-Knight Clinic** \n**Patient:** King **DOB:** 02/01/1966 **Sex:** F **MRN:** 489695\n\n**Encounters:**\n\n**2025-11-20** \n**Reason for Visit:** URI \n**Vitals:** \n- Blood Pressure: 145/80 mmHg \n- Weight: 132 lbs \nMs. King presented with symptoms consistent with an upper respiratory infection, nasal congestion, a non-productive cough, and slight sore throat. There were no signs of fever or lower respiratory tract involvement. She reported fatigue but denied any chest pain, shortness of breath, or sputum production. On examination, her vital signs were stable, and she had mild nasal congestion with no signs of otitis media. A strep test was negative. Given her presentation, I diagnosed an uncomplicated URI and recommended supportive care with hydration, rest, and symptomatic relief with over-the-counter medications as needed.\n\n**2024-10-21** \n**Reason for Visit:** Systemic lupus erythematosus \n**Vitals:** \n- Blood Pressure: 142/92 mmHg \n- Weight: 139 lbs \nMs. King’s follow-up visit for her systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) was uneventful. She reported no new symptoms and continued to feel well-controlled on her current medications, prednisone 5 mg and mycophenolate 500 Her blood pressure was elevated at this visit, but no other signs of lupus flare were present. Physical examination was unremarkable with no joint swelling, rashes, or serositis. Laboratory tests revealed stable renal function and no evidence of active lupus. Given her stable condition, I continued her current regimen and advised her to maintain her regular \n**Reason for Visit:** Systemic lupus erythematosus \n**Vitals:** \n- Blood Pressure: 134/87 mmHg \n- Weight: 143 lbs \nDuring this visit, Ms. King reported feeling generally well with no recent lupus symptoms. She continued on her current treatment prednisone 5 mg and mycophenolate 500 mg, which she has been on since her diagnosis in 2007. Her blood pressure was within normal limits, and she had no complaints of headache, visual changes, or joint pain. Physical examination was unremarkable with no signs of active disease. Laboratory tests showed stable renal and hepatic function, and no signs of infection or other complications. Her condition remains well-controlled, and I recommended continuing her current medications and follow-up visits every three months.\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n\n- **Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE):** The patient’s SLE remains in a controlled state. She is currently on prednisone 5 mg and mycophenolate 500 mg, which have been"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "effective in managing her condition. There are no signs of organ involvement. Ms. King is advised to continue her current medications and follow-up regularly.\n- **Vital Signs:** Her blood pressure readings have been slightly elevated on recent visits but have not warranted any changes in her antihypertensive medications. Her weight has remained relatively stable.\n- **Tobacco Use:** Ms. King is a former smoker who quit in April 2018. She had smoked approximately 1.5 packs per day since starting in August 1998. There are no current signs of nicotine withdrawal or relapse.\n- **Annual Check-ups:** Routine annual physical exams, including blood work and a comprehensive review of her medications, are recommended to ensure continued control of her SLE and overall health.\n- **Patient Education:** Ms. King was educated on the importance of adherence to her medication regimen, regular follow-ups, and lifestyle modifications to manage her condition effectively. She was also advised on the signs and symptoms of lupus flare and when to seek immediate medical attention.\n\n**Social History:**\n\n- **Tobacco Use:** Former smoker, quit in April 2018. Smoked 1.5 packs per day August 1998. Reports honest disclosure and no current use.\n \n**Conclusion:** Ms. King is doing well with her systemic lupus erythematosus managed on her current medication regimen. Her blood pressure has been mildly elevated on recent visits, and she will continue to be monitored for hypertension. There are no signs of active lupus or other complications. She is compliant with her medications and lifestyle modifications. Continued regular follow-ups are recommended to monitor her condition and overall health.\n"
